  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "not stated",
    "relation": "tests",
    "tranche": "T3",
    "tranche_reason": "Tests whether benign fine-tuning erodes safety, using agentic task datasets and agent benchmarks rather than the original fine-tuning setup.",
    "gate_note": "Table 1 and Table 2 report separate pre/post fine-tuning attack-success and refusal rates. Table 3 and Table 20 report effects of safety-oriented response prefixes and suffixes; these do not test whether a compliant opening or adversarial suffix bypasses safety. The paper also reports I-FSJ jailbreak results, but that attack is not a compliant prefill, adversarial suffix, or decoding change as specified in V4.",
    "test_reason": "The study fine-tunes five safety-trained models on benign agentic data (web navigation, code generation) with no harmful examples. It reports pre/post harmful-task attack success and refusal rates (Tables 1–2). This tests V2 in a new setting: agents acting in environments rather than chat. T3 fits better than T2 because the setting, not just the method, is new.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors also conclude that safety-relevant features persist in the fine-tuned agents' representations. Probe analysis points to the initial response token as the decision point, so prefix injection can restore refusal without safety fine-tuning.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Uses supervised fine-tuning on benign web-navigation and code-generation agent datasets, then evaluates agents on agent benchmarks; it does not use the original study's small harmful-example fine-tuning setup.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Effects vary across model and domain. The paper also reports different outcomes for safety-oriented response prefixes and user-prompt suffixes; for closed-source model-based agents, it says suffix injection enhanced safety.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "After fine-tuning, Llama's first-token probability on a harmful web task shifts from refusal tokens ('I' 76.66%) to action tokens ('#' 99.17%). In WebDojo, 100% of aligned-model refusals begin with 'I cannot'.",
        "proposed_claim": "Benign agentic fine-tuning removes safety mainly by displacing refusal-initiating first tokens.",
        "row_ids": []
      },
      {
        "finding": "A refusal-oriented response prefix restores refusal in fine-tuned agents (Table 3; PING), and outperforms an optimized user-prompt suffix (Table 20). Linear probes show safety features persist in the input representations, but the final-token logit turns negative after fine-tuning (Table 16, Fig. 12).",
        "proposed_claim": "Safety-relevant representations survive benign fine-tuning; the failure lies at the first response position and can be reversed by prefilling a refusal.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r17",
          "r18",
          "r19",
          "r20",
          "r21",
          "r22"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "controls": "y",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "https://github.com/HahmDY/agentic-ft-safety.git",
    "peer_reviewed": "AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence",
    "problems": "No uncertainty estimates are reported for the tabulated rates. The paper notes that its linear probes use chat-domain harmful instruction datasets because web-navigation harmful datasets are limited, which may affect probe accuracy.",
    "rows_left_out": "Not applicable; all extracted rows are included.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V2",
        "parts": "Not tested: ordinary chat or instruction data, carefully selected benign data, dose (number of examples), and models other than these five. Only full agentic datasets were used, for one epoch."
      },
      {
        "version": "V1",
        "parts": "No harmful-example fine-tuning."
      },
      {
        "version": "V3",
        "parts": "No aligned-vs-base per-token KL. Figure 2 shows only the first-token distribution for a single example."
      },
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "No compliant prefill, adversarial suffix or decoding attack on the aligned models. I-FSJ is a few-shot jailbreak applied to already fine-tuned agents."
      },
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "No parameter localization."
      },
      {
        "version": "V6",
        "parts": "No test of the cause of shallowness."
      },
      {
        "version": "V7",
        "parts": "No deepening training. PING is an inference-time prefix."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [
      {
        "what": "First-token probability distributions before and after fine-tuning for a harmful task, and the share of refusals that begin with 'I cannot' / 'I can't' (100% on WebDojo, 86% on RedCode).",
        "locator": "Section 2.3, Figure 2",
        "versions": "none (bears on V3/V6 only indirectly)"
      },
      {
        "what": "Benign task success rates before and after fine-tuning (e.g., Llama WebArena 2.42%→22.42%). This is the capability gain that accompanies the safety loss.",
        "locator": "Tables 1-2",
        "versions": "V2 (context)"
      },
      {
        "what": "I-FSJ jailbreak results on fine-tuned agents. For the plain agent, ASR is roughly unchanged, except GLM WebDojo, where it drops to 4.11%.",
        "locator": "Appendix G, Table 13",
        "versions": "none"
      }
    ],
    "verdict_notes": "No uncertainty estimates are given, and decoding is single-run at temperature 0. Refusal is detected by phrase matching. Per-model WebDojo safety sets have 73 harmful tasks, so single-point changes move rates by about 1.4 points. The study cites Qi et al. 2023/2024a as motivation but is not an original source."
  },
